fund-java/15-completablefuture/01-fan-ou.explanation:
Recover each future on its own, then wait for all of them and join in order:

```java
List<CompletableFuture<String>> futures = ids.stream()
    .map(id -> fetch.apply(id).exceptionally(e -> fallback))
    .toList();
return CompletableFuture.allOf(futures.toArray(new CompletableFuture[0]))
    .thenApply(v -> futures.stream().map(CompletableFuture::join).toList());
```

`toList()` materialises the stream, so every fetch starts before `allOf` is built. Putting `exceptionally` on each future (not on the `allOf` result) is what turns one failure into one fallback instead of a failed page.

Moving the recovery onto the combined result instead fails "a failing fetch becomes the fallback", because one failure then replaces every value. Joining each fetch inside the stream rather than after `allOf` runs the calls one after another: four 150 ms fetches took 642 ms, and "the fetches run concurrently" failed. `handle((v, e) -> e == null ? v : fallback)` passes as well. A fetch that throws before it even returns a future is a caller bug, not a remote failure, so it escapes `fetchAll`; "a fetch that throws synchronously is not recovered" checks that. Starting each lookup as `completedFuture(id).thenCompose(fetch)` would swallow it, and "a fetch that throws synchronously is not recovered" then fails.

