fund-java/15-completablefuture/02-bug-hu.explanation:
`exceptionally` on the combined result means one failed fetch fails `allOf`, and the recovery then replaces EVERY value with the fallback. The starter fails "a failing fetch becomes the fallback", which expects `[ok, n/a]`. The other four tests pass, because none of their fetches fails, so the combined recovery never runs. Recover each fetch on its own future before `allOf`, so one failure costs one value, not the page:

```java
List<CompletableFuture<String>> futures = ids.stream()
    .map(id -> fetch.apply(id).exceptionally(e -> fallback))
    .toList();
return CompletableFuture.allOf(futures.toArray(new CompletableFuture[0]))
    .thenApply(v -> futures.stream().map(CompletableFuture::join).toList());
```

Moving the recovery to `allOf(...).exceptionally(e -> null)` before `thenApply` is not a fix: `join` on the failed fetch inside `thenApply` throws a `CompletionException`, and "a failing fetch becomes the fallback" still fails.
